Got it — now I understand the actual use case. Both OpenClaw and Hermes Agent run locally and use messaging platforms like Telegram, Signal, and WhatsApp as their chat interface  (Wikipedia) . The friction is that those platforms actively discourage bot accounts.
OpenClaw already has a built-in WebChat mode — it exposes a Gateway WebSocket at ws://127.0.0.1:18789  (GitHub) , and Hermes Agent also supports a single gateway process that serves CLI, Telegram, Discord, Slack, and WhatsApp all at once  (GitHub) .
So what you actually need is a dedicated WebChat frontend that connects directly to the local agent gateway over WebSocket — completely bypassing Telegram/Signal. I want a Signal-style messaging app, but instead of person-to-person, it's person-to-bot. Clean inbox list, chat view, typing indicators, local persistence. let's build it out. we'll call it OpenChat I'll sell on gumroad for $10. import { useState, useRef, useEffect, useCallback } from "react";

